Short:        Object-level GUI automation for classic AmigaOS
Author:       simond@irrelevant.org (Simon Dick)
Uploader:     simond@irrelevant.org (Simon Dick)
Type:         util/misc
Version:      0.1
Architecture: m68k-amigaos
Distribution: GitHub only (not yet on Aminet)

AmiInspect is a standalone Shell command that walks any window's gadget
tree and prints its roles, labels, classes, IDs, positions, and states -
semantically, not by pixels. It's the platform's first UIA-Inspect /
browser element-picker equivalent: no host machine, server, or session
required, and no coordinate-level guessing that breaks on the next
layout change.

This is the first release of AmiPilot, a project building toward full
object-level GUI automation (find a window/gadget, act on it with
genuinely synthesised input, assert on the result) - AmiInspect is the
read side of that, shipped first because it's independently useful on
its own and is the development tool everything after it gets debugged
against.

Features
--------
  - Prints any window's gadget tree by role, label, class, ID, position,
    and state - target the active window, or any window by a substring
    of its title.
  - Plain GadTools gadgets: correctly distinguishes a checkbox from a
    button even though both produce the identical underlying gadget
    type, using GadTools' own documented attribute-probing contract
    rather than a guess.
  - BOOPSI/ReAction gadgets: reads the real, live class name
    (button.gadget, checkbox.gadget, layout.gadget, and more) via a
    documented NDK mechanism, and maps known classes to a role.
  - Verified against a real, unmodified stock AmigaOS Prefs editor, not
    just software built for this project.
  - Honest, documented limits rather than silent gaps: a few specific,
    well-understood cases (PLACETEXT_IN button labels, a window.class
    window's layout.gadget-nested children) are genuinely unreadable at
    this tier, and AmiInspect says so rather than guessing - see the
    included guide's "Locator Tiers and Limits" page.

Requirements
------------
  - AmigaOS 2.04 (V37) or later. Plain 68000, no FPU - nothing here does
    float work or needs anything newer than a stock CPU.
  - gadtools.library (any version) is optional but recommended - without
    it, AmiInspect can't distinguish a GadTools checkbox from a plain
    button.

Installation
------------
  Copy "AmiInspect" to C: (or anywhere on your command Path). No reboot,
  no configuration.

Quick start
-----------
  AmiInspect                  inspect the active window
  AmiInspect WINDOW=Prefs     inspect the first window whose title
                               contains "Prefs"

AI disclosure
-------------
  Be aware: AmiPilot was written largely by an AI coding agent
  (Anthropic's Claude, via Claude Code), working under human direction,
  review, and on-target testing. Every feature in this release was
  verified on-target under the Copperline emulator against real AmigaOS
  3.2.3 - including catching and fixing a genuine crash bug found only
  by that testing - before being considered done. The entire source is
  BSD-licensed and open for review.

Documentation
-------------
  The full command reference, locator-tier/limits documentation, and
  troubleshooting notes are online at https://sidick.github.io/amipilot/
  and included as amipilot.guide in this archive (AmigaGuide/MultiView,
  OS 2.x+) for offline/on-Amiga reading - same source, both forms.

